{
  "_note": [
    "British/American spelling-variant pairs, unambiguous cases only (no\nprogram/programme, license/licence, practice/practise, metre/meter,\nstory/storey — these have genuine dual meanings or noun/verb splits that\nwould create false positives either direction).",
    "**Data landed ahead of its rule.** Nothing in `revitcheck` imports this\nyet — there is no Revit spelling check built. It lives here rather than\nin the parked PDF/DWG tree (`git checkout pdf-dwg-final`) because the\ncontent is the expensive part and it is not format-specific: every pair\nbelow was either hand-curated or added in response to a real false\npositive on real issued drawings, and a `TextNote` spelling check inside\nRevit will want exactly this list. `config/firm_glossary.json` and\n`config/project_glossary.json` sit alongside it for the same reason —\nsee ARCHIVE-pdf-dwg.md for how they were classified and why the words\nleft *out* of them matter as much as the ones in.",
    "PLANNING.md §1/§4/§10 all call for an **en-GB base dictionary**, and flag\n`pyspellchecker`'s bundled dictionary as US-oriented — confirmed directly:\nits default 'en' dictionary flags \"specialised\", \"colour\", \"kerb\",\n\"centre\", \"programme\" as unknown. A full self-hosted LanguageTool server\n(§10's actual recommended choice for this) needs a JVM + downloaded\nlanguage models not available in this dev environment, so this is a\npragmatic interim fix: layer a curated variant list on top of a general\ndictionary rather than block on standing up LanguageTool. Swapping to a\nproper en-GB dictionary (LanguageTool, or a hunspell en_GB `.dic`) is\nreal follow-up work this list doesn't try to replace — it covers common\ncases, not every word in the language.",
    "Maps BRITISH -> AMERICAN. Both directions get used:\n- British forms are added to the accepted-words set so they never flag.\n- American forms found in text get flagged with the British correction as\n  `suggested_fix` (PLANNING.md §8: `Spelling: <correct word>`), which is\n  the actual point of the requirement — catching drafters defaulting to\n  American spelling, not just suppressing false positives on British ones.",
    "Data fix on conversion to JSON, 2026-09-07: the archived Python built these three by stripping three characters from the -ogue form (`word[:-3]`), one too many, producing 'catalo'/'dialo'/'analo' - the source comment stated the intended 'catalogue' -> 'catalog' that the code did not produce, and no test covered it. The consequence was real in both directions: the correct American spellings 'catalog'/'dialog'/'analog' would never have been recognised, so the check would have failed to flag exactly the words it exists to catch."
  ],
